) is defined as PEB(p) = sqrt(tr(J(p)^{-1})), where J(p) is the Fisher information matrix of the RTT measurement model with respect to p (after marginalising or jointly estimating b). For UEs whose horizontal location lies near the projection of the satellite's orbital ground track, the geometric dilution of precision (GDOP) degenerates and PEB(p) becomes very large, so that although the median PEB over a uniform UE distribution satisfies a 10 km verification accuracy target, the mean PEB and worst-case PEB are an order of magnitude larger.\n\nDesign a single-LEO RTT-based positioning and verification procedure—comprising the choice of measurement epochs {t_i}, an estimator p̂ of p, and a verification test deciding whether a claimed location is consistent with the RTT measurements—that meets the following requirements simultaneously:\n\n(i) the median horizontal positioning error over a uniform distribution of UEs on the visible portion of Earth is at most 10 km;\n\n(ii) the fraction of UEs (including those located near the sub-satellite orbital track where GDOP is poor) whose horizontal positioning error exceeds 10 km is bounded by a prescribed small ε > 0;\n\n(iii) the procedure uses only one LEO satellite (no multi-satellite fusion).\n\nAlternatively, prove that no such procedure exists, i.e., establish a fundamental lower bound showing that for any single-LEO RTT estimator the worst-case PEB on a positive-measure set of UE locations must exceed 10 km whenever the median PEB meets the 10 km target.","status_search_result":"The problem is posed as an open research question concerning how to mitigate severe outliers in single-LEO RTT-based location verification when geometric dilution of precision is poor. No publication was identified that resolves the question for the strict single-LEO setting; subsequent work on NTN/LEO localization typically proposes multi-LEO or hybrid solutions rather than guaranteeing the worst-case 10 km target with a single satellite.","status_evidence":[{"title":"NTN-based 6G Localization: Vision, Role of LEOs, and Open Problems","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.12259","snippet":"Although it is not clear how to handle such users if single LEO positioning is used... this makes a strong case for multi-LEO positioning.","claim":"The question of how to handle severe-GDOP outlier UEs under single-LEO RTT positioning is explicitly identified as unresolved."}]},"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"2ebd2732-0072-4d20-933f-aef47827b827"}
